- The system has an initial stock (12 in this case). 
- There is a target_time = 100. knowledge_cutoff = 100. horizon_time = 130.
- There are many reports (historical events) with event_time (when the event occurred) and received_time (when the report was received). Also revision numbers: each event_id may appear multiple times with different revisions; highest visible revision (i.e., the revision with max revision number among those with received_time <= knowledge_cutoff) for each event_id is to be kept. Also: we first filter reports with received_time <= knowledge_cutoff. Then keep highest revision for each event_id among those. Then we keep chosen events with event_time <= target_time. That is, we apply events that occurred at event_time <= target_time, but only those that have a visible report (post-knowledge cutoff filtering). Then, we apply these events in order of (event_time, event_id) (likely lexicographic for ties). Starting from initial_stock = 12.

- Stock changes rules:

  - Receipts add quantity to stock: stock += quantity.
  - Demands consume min(stock, quantity); any unmet quantity is lost. So if quantity > stock, we lose (quantity - stock). Stock becomes 0 after consumption. If demand is zero quantity, nothing changes and no lost. Unmet quantity is considered "lost". Stockout is defined as there exists strictly positive unmet demand (i.e., quantity > stock pre-demand). So we need to track total lost quantity historically through target_time (total historical_lost). Also maybe track stock at target_time after applying events.

- After historical reconstruction, we apply events in the future_schedule (these are known hypothetical events) from the reconstructed stock. But we must include events up to horizon_time (130). The future events go up to event_time = 118. There may be no events beyond that. The horizon_time may be after last future event; but we still need to compute stock_at_horizon after processing both the historical and future events up to horizon_time. Likely just after processing all future events (since horizon_time is 130 and future events max time 118). No more events after 118, so we hold final stock after last event.

- Then we compute future_lost, which is the total lost quantity from future events only (i.e., from future_schedule only). Not including historical lost.

- Also we need first_future_stockout_time: the earliest future event time with unmet demand (i.e., where demand > stock) in the scenario. If no future demand is unmet, return -1.

- Output five integers: stock_at_target, historical_lost (through target_time), stock_at_horizon (after all events up to horizon_time, i.e., after future schedule), future_lost, first_future_stockout_time.

